From 949795652c16072619d4a33ba72edd00b5e183b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ui): share native scene rendering and GUI actors

Share bounded scene, layout, image, text and actor rendering primitives before registering domain-specific forms and containers.

Original native 1.26.51.1/protocol2193 execution binds the complete
visibility setter and dependency scheduler: hidden computed dimensions
reset to zero without removing authored properties or collection entries.
Use those metrics for child and peer sizes, preserve disabled dimensions,
and default stack cross-axis sizes to the parent extent. This prevents
inactive factory templates from consuming title and category-list space.

Render native left/top/right/bottom image borders and signed undersized
regions with metadata-scaled float UVs through the normal Java GUI path.
The bounded layout solver and alpha lifecycle still need wider
native comparison. Native GPU winding, font metrics, UV/color tracks
remain explicit parity boundaries.

Match the pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 ImageComponent default layout: image skins
without explicit dimensions inherit the owner extent; texture aspect sizing
only applies when default_size_scales_to_ratio is enabled. This keeps slider
tracks and thumbs at their authored sizes instead of their PNG pixel sizes.
All 27 layout tests and five captured Settings sliders at two viewports pass
CPU verification. Actual GPU rendering remains a separate live check.

Original-machine CPU controls bind progress endpoint/color rules, quintic
flight timing and matched loss-to-gain state comparison, typed lock reads
and gamerule gating. Native component-marker anchor preference, Java item
equivalence, cursor flight domain and actual GPU/Iris drawing remain
explicit parity boundaries. Private tests exercise current-menu ownership,
vanilla dispatch, replacement cleanup, scroll clipping and renderer gates.

Render admitted real player portraits using negotiated signed native UID
identity and retained GUI-only controller, equipment and emote playback.
Do not alter world clocks, variables or effect dispatch. Missing actors
produce no portrait; unverified classes, opacity and active GUI effect
submission keep ordinary controls. Use the pinned Mouse camera and the
normal Java entity GUI pipeline with independent depth scaling.

Parse fixed grid capacity separately from bounded pixel geometry. Sparse
content measurement visits only actual bounded children, so authored
million-cell capacities neither allocate that capacity nor reject an
otherwise valid scene. Fixed-grid sizing and native GPU projection remain
explicit comparison boundaries.

Measure authored no-template manual grid children against the full parent
and place them at their declared grid_position cell stride. The original
fresh native scheduler retains full-parent percentage extents even when
the authored grid declares a million columns. Template grids keep their
existing collection placement and measurement path.

Use the native absent grid_position default[0,0] for all no-template grids,
without inventing sequential placement for unspecified manual coordinates.
The original reader/writer controls bind zero/missing dimensions to[0,0].
Their secondary inactivity bit lifecycle remains unbound, so visible
zero-capacity manual grids retain the ordinary fallback instead of being
silently replaced by one-cell geometry. Checked index overflow is a Java
work-limit fallback, not a native exception semantics claim.

Admit an authored manual subset only through the negotiated count-one
request capability. Preserve complete template chest coverage and the
ordinary legacy route. Wait for real contents on the same live menu and
for server activation acknowledgment before dispatching native actions.

Bind each action to its actual menu slot, clipped pointer rectangle and
presentation lifetime. Reject stale or reused menus and consume unsupported
keyboard and drag mutations instead of redirecting them to generic pickup.
Restore ordinary controls deterministically after presentation, transport
or scroll refresh failure.

Exclude ignored branches from layout extents, stack spacing and fill allocation.

Match pinned native stack placement: suppress child offsets and apply anchors only when use_child_anchors is enabled. This keeps oversized Loot cards below their header while retaining ordinary panel anchors; targeted layout controls cover both stack orientations and nested panels.

Use the inherited authored scissor as the portrait intermediate viewport.
The pinned native live actor renderer uses the control width and center
in a full-screen projection; it does not crop to the custom control.
Compensate the larger Java viewport center without changing the camera
units, model origin or parent clipping. Projection tests cover GUI scales,
pitches and model points. Exact visual-model framing remains a live gate.

Match the native 1.26.51.1 GUI player origin before pitch rotation.
A live CubeCraft portrait draw through 14558da04..14558dfa1 sets the skip
flag, reads height 0 and preserves the control-center translation. World
player eye height 1.62001 and the world model-root translation do not apply
to this GUI context. Cancel Java's Living/Avatar origin shift instead.
Preserve authored scale and inherited scissor. Projection tests cover
player scales and pitch; matched-cursor native and Java captures align
the Steve head within one image pixel at the same 3840 x 2134 viewport.
Custom costume geometry,
alternate postures and broader actor classes remain separate gates.
Use transported fractional TTF line advances for measurement and drawing
so native display scaling does not overlap wrapped labels.

Render non-player portrait geometry with GUI-only appearance, controller,
playback and material state. Resolve accepted packs over the licensed
matching native library, including PNG, JPEG and TGA image precedence.
Use transported raw actor bounds and authored portrait offsets. Preserve
native X/Y projection while converting the depth axis to Java's
orthographic GUI convention. World animation clocks and effect consumers
do not supply GUI playback. Active GUI sound/particle effects and broader
renderer lifetime limits remain separate parity gates.

Preserve transparent image owners' opaque children unless propagate_alpha
is enabled. Measure percentage template-grid children against the full
grid owner and use collection rows for default content height. Hide named
scroll controls when content fits unless scrollbar_always_visible is set,
without changing authored visibility or unrelated controls with the same
name. Native short-dialogue execution establishes the absent scrollbar;
boundary tests retain overflowing and explicitly persistent controls.

Honor enabled_newline in authored edit boxes with retained multiline text
editing, wrapping, selection, bounded input and clipped internal scrolling.
Keep the original single-line widget for other fields. Resize and dependent
scene refreshes preserve both selection directions and caret identity
without manufacturing value changes. Route IME composition and its input
area through the target client while the authored scene owns decorations.

Unmodified native BDS traffic establishes incremental name/dialogue edits,
empty editor scenes, action modes, full-list action flushes, command LF
and cmd_ver 52 serialization, and no student opening/closing execution.
Retail hides Add URL; education-specific URL editing and controller/TTS
branches remain unverified rather than established by packet tests.

Keep a separate picture renderer and GPU texture per native actor context.
Java 26.3 otherwise prepares all actors into one shared texture before
GUI blits, making every picker entry display the last submitted skin.
Release unused pictures after preparation and all pictures on renderer
close. Private direct GPU comparison confirms five distinct variants.

Direct and ViaProxy BDS checks retain edited names and skin choices.
The proxy also retains multiline dialogue, command text and command mode
when the teacher scene closes and reopens. Resolve text color from the
edit box's authored text_control, rather than assigning Java white to a
native white dialogue bubble. Size single-line fields before assigning
their value and reflow horizontal scrolling without moving selection.
The initial leading-character regression and retained selection pass.

Use Bedrock GuiData scale for the actor camera correction. Java HUD
scale only controls the final texture projection, so changing it must
not move a native portrait within its authored control.

Render the core-resolved title factory through the existing native UI renderer. Keep its title clock across subtitle updates, follow alpha/wait chains, and release resources on clear or disconnect. The accepted CubeCraft 0.0.55 HUD supplies the banner and subtitle icon rather than Java title text. Preserve ordinary title packets for clients without the native HUD channel.

Render independent action-bar factory scenes through the shared native HUD
renderer. Let their authored alpha/wait animations destroy named controls,
resolving each target inside its control ancestry and factory instance.
Retain sibling instances and omit null optional numeric bindings consistently
with the animation sampler. Packet-timed titles keep their separate lifetime;
disconnects clear both channels and owned resources.

Native build 51061372/protocol 2193 confirms a six-second constant-alpha
override and a persistent one-second override without destruction. Empty
plain and text-object packets, title Clear and Reset preserve the caption.
Direct and ViaProxy replay screenshots verify visible six-second expiry
and replacement. Unrelated missing-world actor checks still fail, so these
captures verify the HUD only. Full add-on tests and builds pass. Targeted
regressions cover sequential and independent tracks, null delay bindings,
the destruction boundary, sibling isolation, persistent clocks and cleanup.
Full HUD scaling, resource reloads,
font/GPU comparisons and synchronized opacity preferences remain open.

Use the shared native easing evaluator for all JSON UI alpha curves. The
pinned 1.26.51.1/build 51061372 constructor selects the same 32 functions
as camera easing. Its alpha updater clamps progress, retains interpolation
overshoot and uses linear for an unknown selector. Preserve the native
exponential endpoint instead of forcing exact zero.

All 3,552 samples executed through the original alpha updater match the
production Track evaluator within 1.2e-7. Focused regressions cover all
curves, opacity clamping, overshoot and unknown selectors; full add-on tests
pass. Other animation types and remaining GUI families still require
implementation and native comparison.

Direct and ViaProxy replay screenshots retain the fading caption, expiry
and replacement without an easing fallback. Missing actor-rendering checks
still fail in the recorded scene. Native screenshot timing and complete
visible curve comparison remain unverified.

Preserve named alpha/wait animation graphs, including cycles, shared
successors and namespace-relative references. Keep per-control clocks
through hover changes. Scroll input sends authored play/reset events;
repeated play preserves elapsed time and reset restores the waiting state.
Use the same manager for sampling and destruction, so event-gated controls
do not expire from screen age. A successful destruction ends its chain.

Pinned 1.26.51.1/build 51061372 manager execution establishes same-frame
successor carry, exact loop boundaries, render gates and terminal behavior.
All 256 mixed native event/frame steps match production within 1e-6.
Targeted graph and lifecycle regressions and the full add-on suite pass.
Java partial-tick retreat during pause retains the last animation time.
Color, UV and flipbook output, complete input-event delivery
and fresh direct/proxy visual comparisons remain open parity requirements.

Render supported persistent accepted-pack HUD root scenes separately from timed title/actionbar overlays. Retain bounded scene payloads and suppress vanilla HUD replacements only when the accepted authored scene supplies the corresponding decoration. Core root-scene and layout tests validate the resolved authored scene.

The pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build51061372 color updater at 144578f30
interpolates image RGBA independently; its reset callback 1445791a0
restores the starting color. An absent image component finishes without
consuming time. Factory/parser 14457d220/14040e170 uses white defaults,
including omitted array channels, and native named color constants.

A private comparison executes the unchanged native updater across 768
frames covering all 32 easing selectors, boundary times, zero duration,
negative channels and overshoot. Production output differs by at most
1.1920929e-7. Targeted tests cover resets, successors, refreshes and final
opacity composition. This closes the image color-track implementation
gap; full UI family and native/direct/proxy visual verification remains.

Sample source-pixel UV tracks and native flip_book state through the shared
image renderer. Use actual texture extents, preserve authored crop on the
other axis, and retain clocks across binding and texture refreshes. Each
render frame updates a control manager once even when layout and extraction
both inspect it. Preserve native loop, reversal, reset and successor rules.

Pinned 1.26.51.1/build51061372 updater 14457b900 interpolates UV with the
shared easing functions and duration floor. Flipbook updater 144579600
consumes at most one frame interval per call, floors FPS at 0.1, ignores
frame_step, and uses a 1/256 pixel origin inset and 1/128 pixel crop inset.
Reset 1445798c0 retains reverse direction and restores zero UV. The native
manager holds a non-looping terminal frame while resettable is true; only
a non-resettable terminal track finishes and may start a successor.

Private unchanged-native execution covers 768 UV samples across all 32
easing selectors, with maximum source-pixel error 3.8146973e-6, and 90
flipbook updater/manager steps with matching states and crop coordinates.
Targeted stateful tests cover clipping, texture resize, refresh clocks,
reversal, resets and successors. Aseprite tracks and complete GUI-family
interaction, native pixels and direct/proxy verification remain required.

An isolated software-GPU run draws four UV positions and four flipbook
frames through the production scene renderer. All eight sampled colors
match exactly. Both full suites pass: 1,105 core tests and 857 add-on tests,
with no failures. The broader replay fixture stopped before all packets
were sent; these pixels establish texture selection only.

Measure native labels from styled paragraphs, retaining actual newlines,
empty lines and literal escape sequences. Use the longest paragraph for
intrinsic width instead of measuring the complete multiline text as one
line. This keeps title and subtitle backgrounds at the authored extents.
The accepted protocol-2193 CubeCraft HUD explicitly declares these
backgrounds; retain their authored opacity rather than removing them.

Retain native animation clocks and wait-for-render state across HUD binding
refreshes. Cache each HUD layout until its scene, viewport, font metrics,
image revision or animated dimensions change. Reuse wrapped label lines,
release text caches when controls retire or scene handling fails, and avoid
scheduler allocations
for controls with no active tracks. Skip invisible draw submissions.
Resource reload apply callbacks retire HUD font and image caches without
resetting scene clocks. Targeted paragraph, wrapping, cache invalidation
and lifecycle regressions plus the complete add-on test suite pass.
Native GPU/font comparisons and client opacity preferences remain separate
verification boundaries.

Play Aseprite UI tracks using transported atlas metadata and native elapsed
millisecond frame selection. Keep phase across reset events and binding
refreshes, stop phase advancement while metadata is absent, and scale crop
extents against each physical texture axis while retaining frame origins.
All shared image renderers use the same animation state and source region.

Tests retain real menu objects and exercise pickup, quick moves and drag
input across every implemented storage family. A separate pinned-asset
check exercises the visible-control gate. Complete native/direct/proxy
visual and transaction comparisons remain required.

Bound persistent HUD image metadata with least-recently-used eviction. The previous hard limit rejected valid later icons and retired their title scenes after 256 textures. Keep pack texture ownership in the resource manager and cover eviction, frequent hits and replaced resource sources.

Default ordinary image controls to keep_ratio, matching the Bedrock 1.26.51.1 image loader and aspect-fit draw path. Preserve explicit false, slicing, clipping, signed destinations, and source regions. Tests compare default fitting against authored stretching for rectangular images.

Treat an unavailable or corrupt synchronous pack image as a presentation
failure instead of silently omitting it. Container item extraction can
otherwise continue without its authored panel or slot images. Existing
screen failure handling retains the authoritative ordinary menu. Keep
remote URL loading asynchronous and preserve its pending-image behavior.
A failing regression covers missing resources, cached decode failures and
recovery after a new pack source supplies valid pixels. This verifies the
asset-failure path; it does not identify the exact report cause or establish
native inventory pixels at every GUI scale.

Treat recipe action annotations as interactive controls independently of
close commands. Select their authored hover and locked branches using the
shared presentation path. A CPU regression covers both enabled states and
pointer states without adding a synthetic close annotation.

Treat native toggle controls as interactive independently of form response
annotations. Their checked/unchecked hover and locked branches must remain
available to authored inventory groups. The presentation regression covers
all eight checked, enabled and pointer states without a form input marker.
Native device and pixel equivalence remain separate verification gates.

Preserve authored child order in layout and sort only the complete draw
list by cumulative layer. Native 1.26.51.1 traversal1445ba980 visits
children in vector order and adds each authored layer without a depth
increment. Sorting siblings first reversed equal-layer draws across
nested subtrees, hiding the cooking tab icons behind their backgrounds.
Use reverse authored order to resolve equal-layer action overlap.

Twelve original traversal controls bind cumulative layers, child order
and inherited visibility. Nested-image and installed-screen pointer
regressions both fail with the previous behavior and pass with this fix.
This does not establish native GPU batching, pixels or full UI parity.

Use native integer-or-boolean component flag reads, including unsigned
64-bit values. Real values and strings retain the property default. The
original FocusComponent factory disables absent focus_enabled, so shared
actions must not acquire keyboard traversal focus by default. Apply the
same flag reader during scene visibility admission and runtime queries.
This does not replace Java navigation with the full native focus manager.

Use native directional edge scoring, binary32 geometry, registration-order
ties, explicit identifiers and stop overrides for shared-screen arrows.
Apply nearest clipping ancestors and viewport wrapping rather than Java
widget rectangle traversal. Preserve editable-control key handling and
retained widget identities. Screen invokes its superclass focus path
directly, so route declined arrow keys through the native selector before
changing focus.

Search original ScrollView viewport subtrees before the global clipped
search, preserving subtree order and the native no-wrap fallback. Apply
FocusContainer stop/contained/custom policies, node-name custom routes,
recursive remembered focus and the native 50-entry weak history. Explicit
focus identifiers bypass those container policies. Retain history while
scene properties refresh on existing widgets and keep offscreen edit
controls eligible for scroll navigation.

Original component constructors, custom-property parsing, ancestor
registration, viewport weak binding, subtree iteration, directional
selection and destruction execute against the pinned native binary.
The bounded selector matches 9,320 native steps; original history controls
also verify disabled-control fallback and the 50-entry limit. Preserve
native control/screen construction, non-widget controls, scroll physics,
full input routing and physical device translation as separate gates.

Synchronize widget visibility and editability during viewport reflow so a
newly revealed edit field accepts input before another render pass. Keep
animation advancement outside draw collection to avoid re-entering layout.
A screen regression traverses action and edit controls beyond the clip,
types into the selected field and verifies the original input index.
